# What a pattern dictionary is, which of its entries this interpreter
# reads, and where it and the specification differ.
#
# PLRM 4.9.1: "All pattern dictionaries contain an entry named
# PatternType, whose value identifies the kind of pattern the dictionary
# describes: type 1 denotes a tiling pattern, type 2 a shading pattern."
# There are exactly two, and no PLRM table defines any other value.
#
# A pattern is also a colour space -- the Pattern family of PLRM 4.8 --
# so this family and the colour space family are held to each other:
# tests/colourspace-facts crosses an uncoloured pattern over every base
# space, and this register asks what the pattern itself must be. Neither
# list is the other's, and the defect each was written after was a
# member absent from both.
#
# Membership is DERIVED: the guard offers every code from -3 to 20, and
# the non-integer forms, and calls a type accepted when a pattern built
# with it PAINTS. A third type implemented shows up whether or not
# anyone remembered to say so.
#
#   <type>  <accepted|refused>  <errorname>  <reason>

1  accepted  none  a tiling pattern, whose cells are painted by a procedure and stepped over the region, PLRM Table 4.9
2  accepted  none  a shading pattern, which paints the region through a shading dictionary, PLRM Table 4.10

types 2

# What a value that is not a pattern type gets, and why the three
# answers differ from one another.
#
# Practice differs on two of these, so PLRM decides and the divergence is
# recorded rather than hidden: Table 4.9 types the entry as an integer, so
# a value of another type is a type error and only an integer can be out
# of range.
#
#   badtype <case>  <errorname>

badtype absent       undefined
badtype zero         rangecheck
badtype negative     rangecheck
badtype three        rangecheck
badtype twenty       rangecheck
badtype real         typecheck
badtype name         typecheck
badtype string       typecheck

badtypes 8

# The entries each type carries, and what this does with each.
#
# Which are required is PROBED, one pattern per entry, by leaving that
# entry out. An entry that quietly becomes optional, or quietly starts
# being demanded, fails here rather than being discovered by a program.
#
#   entry <type> <name>  <required|optional>  <errorname|none>
#
# The error name is what leaving it out raises. PLRM's makepattern entry
# lists undefined among its errors and a required entry that is absent
# is exactly that case; a value present but outside its range is
# rangecheck.

entry 1 PatternType     required  undefined
entry 1 PaintType       required  undefined
entry 1 TilingType      required  undefined
entry 1 BBox            required  undefined
entry 1 XStep           required  undefined
entry 1 YStep           required  undefined
entry 1 PaintProc       required  undefined
entry 1 XUID            optional  none
entry 2 PatternType     required  undefined
entry 2 Shading         required  undefined
entry 2 XUID            optional  none

entries 11

# The values an entry may hold, where PLRM states a rule and a value
# outside it would otherwise be painted or silently dropped.
#
#   value <entry> <case>  <takes|refuses>

value XStep      zero      refuses
value YStep      zero      refuses
value XStep      negative  takes
value PaintType  three     refuses
value PaintType  one       takes
value PaintType  two       takes
value TilingType four      refuses
value TilingType one       takes

values 8

# What is true of the dictionary makepattern hands back, as opposed to
# the one the program supplied.
#
# PLRM 8.2: "Finally, makepattern makes the new dictionary read-only and
# returns it." That is not a courtesy. The instantiated pattern is this
# interpreter's statement that the dictionary has been checked, and every
# later site relies on it; a program able to rewrite the type afterwards
# would reach machinery chosen for a type the dictionary no longer has,
# and be told about the failure by whichever internal helper got there.
#
#   instantiated <property>  <holds|absent>

instantiated read-only    holds
instantiated implementation holds

instantiateds 2

# Where this interpreter and the specification differ, and what is to
# become of each difference. A difference the guard can find and this
# list does not name is a failure; so is a line here naming a difference
# the guard can no longer find.
#
#   <what>  <settled|thorn|heading>  <reason>


xuid-unread  settled  PLRM Table 4.9 offers XUID as a way for a pattern to carry a globally unique identity. Nothing here consults it, and a pattern is accepted with or without one. The identity a cached pattern is filed under is a serial this interpreter issues, for the same reason a form's is: an identifier the program supplies may be shared between two patterns, survives a copy, and may collide with one this interpreter chose. The entry is accepted so a program written for the specification is not turned away

absent-entry-differs-by-operator  settled  A pattern dictionary carrying no PatternType is undefined at makepattern and a type error at setpattern, and that is deliberate. makepattern is handed a prototype to check, and PLRM Table 4.9 marks the entry required, so its absence is a missing entry. setpattern is handed something that must ALREADY be a pattern: PLRM 8.2 defines it as an equivalence for setcolorspace and setcolor, gives it no error list of its own, and says its operand is a dictionary instantiated by makepattern -- so a dictionary with no type is not a deficient pattern but the wrong kind of operand. Practice splits on this, one way and the other, and the specification leaves the name open; this interpreter also holds every operator to refusing an operand it cannot use with a type error, which decides it. One place knows the vocabulary and the caller states which fault it is reporting

divergences 2
